The Nautilus DevOps team is currently engaged in a cleanup process, focusing on removing unnecessary data and services from their AWS account. 
As part of the migration process, several resources were created for one-time use only, necessitating a cleanup effort to optimize their AWS environment.

Delete an IAM group named iamgroup_ammar using terraform. Make sure to keep the provisioning code, as we might need to provision this instance again later.

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform.

Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
📋 Steps
1. Destroy only the EC2 instance (keeps the code in main.tf):

bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
terraform destroy -target=aws_instance.ec2 -auto-approve
This will:

Delete the nautilus-ec2 instance in AWS.

Remove it from the Terraform state.

Leave the resource "aws_instance" "ec2" { ... } block in main.tf untouched, so you can re-provision it later.

2. Verify the instance is in terminated state:

bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-instances \
  --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=nautilus-ec2" \
  --query "Reservations[].Instances[].State.Name"
Expected output:

json
[
    "terminated"
]
If the instance is fully removed from LocalStack, the query returns an empty list — that also confirms deletion.

3. Confirm the provisioning code is still present:

bash
grep -n "nautilus-ec2" main.tf
You should still see your original resource "aws_instance" "ec2" block.

💡 Why -target and Not terraform destroy
Command	Effect
terraform destroy -target=aws_instance.ec2	Deletes only the instance; main.tf is unchanged ✅
terraform destroy	Destroys all resources and may prompt you to delete the config ❌
Deleting the block from main.tf	Removes the provisioning code ❌
The task explicitly requires you to keep the provisioning code, so targeted destroy is the correct approach.

⚠️ If Terraform Says the Resource Is Not in State
If the instance exists in AWS but isn't tracked by Terraform (e.g., created outside this config), import it first:

bash
# Get the instance ID
INSTANCE_ID=$(aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-instances \
  --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=nautilus-ec2" \
  --query "Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId" --output text)

# Import into state, then destroy
terraform import aws_instance.ec2 $INSTANCE_ID
terraform destroy -target=aws_instance.ec2 -auto-approve
✅ Final Check Before Submitting
bash
terraform state list
aws_instance.ec2 should not appear in the list (it's been removed from state), while main.tf still contains the resource block. This satisfies both requirements: the instance is deleted, and the provisioning code is preserved.

